<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="serine racemization" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>|FRAME: SER| biosynthesis is a major metabolic pathway in many organisms. Its end product, |FRAME: SER|, is not only used in protein synthesis, but also as a precursor for the biosynthesis of glycine, cysteine, tryptophan, and phospholipids. In addition, it directly or indirectly serves as a source of one-carbon units for the biosynthesis of various compounds. Many organisms from all domain of life are able to convert |FRAME: SER| to |FRAME: D-SERINE| by racemization. In bacteria racemization usually takes place for the sake of degradation. Some organisms can use |FRAME: D-SERINE| as the sole carbon and nitrogen sources (see |FRAME: PWY0-1535|). More recently it has been shown that the ability to utilize and/or detoxify D-serine plays a role in pathogenesis |CITS: [12823810][17088369][17785472][19674114]|. In other cases racemization evolved as an antibiotic resistance mechanism. Resistance to vancomycin that originated in |FRAME: TAX-1350| (collectively called VRE, vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus), but eventually spread to other organisms depends on D-ala-D-ala ligases with altered specificity that incorporate either |FRAME: D-LACTATE| or |FRAME: D-SERINE| instead of the second |FRAME: D-ALANINE|. When incorporated into peptidoglycan, these modified peptides do not bind glycopeptide antibiotics |CITS: [10209740][10878136]|. D-serine was thought to exist only in bacteria until it was found to serve as a signaling molecule in the vertebrate brain. D-Serine, which is synthesized in neurons by the bifunctional serine racemase/D-serine dehydratase, serves as a neuromodulator by coactivating NMDA receptors, making them able to open if they then also bind glutamate. D-serine is a potent agonist at the glycine site (NR1) of the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA)-type glutamate receptor (NMDAR). For the receptor to open, glutamate and either glycine or D-serine must bind to it |CITS: [20106978]|.</Description>
